text:
Soon Aluda cast his eye on the trail of the Kists.  
He shifted swiftly; the gun gave a crack:  
one thievish Gilgveli had a bad moment;  
he falls from his horse, hanging head-down by the neck;  
the bullet wound is at the top of his shoulder, and fire seems to seize him there;  
the Gilgveli, slain by his comrade, is dragging the trigger.  
Mutsal’s gun burst forth; the cliff’s edge would split apart,  
and the leaden bullet, sought out, would fall into Aluda’s lap.

II. The sun has risen in the sky, the mists have descended into the ravines;  
The giants of the Caucasus have gathered at the edge of the sky.  
The hawks, the bird-chasers, have set out for the hunt;  
The eagles are being pursued by the falcons, their attendants for eating at no cost.

I could not bring myself to cut off my right hand;  
my heart grew angry with me, saying, “Do not do what is to be done with difficulty:  
‘Let my name be diminished; I prefer you as my counselor.’”

The new clothes of the Khevsurs turn to ash,  
they look at Aluda with hostility,  
they shout at him angrily:

“Die, death would be better for you, since you are a liar;  
take off the snake-mouthed one, throw it to the women as a spindle;  
the shield — for stamping cloth, the rifle — hang it outside;  
the pistol is only fit to be spun like a spindle!  
You have run away from the Kist’s son, you have turned into a woman.  
You killed him, yet you did not cut off his right hand; why did you follow him from behind?!”

III. It grew dark, the waters are weeping, the hem of night burns us;  
It is the time of the stars’ twinkling, of dew falling upon the grass,  
of the dead souls rising from the grave, the time of their quiet singing.

“Do not make my burden lighter, if God’s grace is upon you,  
for I too am a servant of Gudani Cross,  
a Khevsur of your water;  
as if we are one, Berdia,  
dwellers of one mountain.”
